<exercise label="ex-binomial-colorado-energy-realistic" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Binomial Distributions: Colorado Energy Conservation Audits</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Many utility companies promote environmental sustainability and energy conservation by offering discount rates to consumers who keep their monthly energy usage below certain established subsidy standards. A recent state environmental report notes that 35% of residential energy consumers in Colorado have reduced their home electricity usage sufficiently to qualify for these discounted rates. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Suppose an energy inspector randomly selects a sample of 5 residential subscribers from Denver, Colorado. Let <m>X</m> denote the number of chosen subscribers who qualify for the favorable discounted rates. 
        </p>

    </introduction>

    <!-- Part A: Probability Calculation (At least 4) -->
    <task xml:id="task-colorado-energy-prob-at-least-4">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the probability that at least four of the five randomly selected residential subscribers qualify for the favorable discounted energy rates.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin  answer="0.0540" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Because we are drawing a fixed sample of independent residential households (<m>n = 5</m>) where each household has a constant, binary success probability of qualifying (<m>p = 0.35</m>), the random variable <m>X</m> follows a standard Binomial distribution: <m>X \sim \text{Binomial}(n = 5, p = 0.35)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The query asks for the probability that <term>at least four</term> households qualify, which corresponds to the sum of the disjoint discrete point probabilities for exactly 4 and exactly 5 successes:
                <me>P(X \ge 4) = P(X = 4) + P(X = 5)</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Evaluating each point using the standard Binomial probability mass function formula, <m>P(X = k) = \binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}</m>:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Exactly 4 Qualify:</strong>
                            <me>P(X = 4) = \binom{5}{4} (0.35)^4 (0.65)^1 = 5 \cdot 0.015006 \cdot 0.65 \approx 0.04877</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Exactly 5 Qualify:</strong>
                            <me>P(X = 5) = \binom{5}{5} (0.35)^5 (0.65)^0 = 1 \cdot 0.005252 \cdot 1 \approx 0.00525</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                Summing these individual probabilities together yields the cumulative interval area:
                <me>P(X \ge 4) = 0.04877 + 0.00525 = 0.05402</me>
                Rounded to four decimal places, the final probability that at least four households qualify for the favorable rates is exactly <strong>0.0540</strong> (or a 5.40% chance).
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Second Probability Computation Task (Fewer than 2) -->
    <task xml:id="task-colorado-energy-prob-fewer-than-2">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the probability that fewer than two of the five randomly selected residential subscribers qualify for the favorable discounted energy rates.
            </p>
            <p>
                Probability = <fillin answer="0.4284" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The query asks for the probability that <term>fewer than two</term> households qualify, which corresponds to the sum of the disjoint discrete point probabilities for exactly 0 and exactly 1 success:
                <me>P(X \lt 2) = P(X = 0) + P(X = 1)</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Evaluating each point using the Binomial probability mass function formula:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Exactly 0 Qualify:</strong>
                            <me>P(X = 0) = \binom{5}{0} (0.35)^0 (0.65)^5 = 1 \cdot 1 \cdot 0.11603 \approx 0.11603</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <strong>Exactly 1 Qualifies:</strong>
                            <me>P(X = 1) = \binom{5}{1} (0.35)^1 (0.65)^4 = 5 \cdot 0.35 \cdot 0.17851 \approx 0.31239</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                Summing these individual probabilities together yields the cumulative interval area:
                <me>P(X \lt 2) = 0.11603 + 0.31239 = 0.42842</me>
                Rounded to four decimal places, the final probability that fewer than two households qualify for the favorable rates is exactly <strong>0.4284</strong> (or a 42.84% chance).
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part C: Expected Value and Variance -->
        <!-- Part C: Expected Value and Variance (Converted to Multiple Choice with Interpretation) -->
    <task xml:id="task-colorado-energy-moments-mc">
        <statement>

            <p>
                Calculate the expected number and the variance of qualifying households within a random sample of  <m>5</m> subscribers. Which option correctly identifies these values and delivers the most accurate  interpretation of their meaning?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>E(X) = 3.50</m> households, <m>\text{Var}(X) = 1.05</m>. This indicates that a clear majority of the sampled households will reliably qualify for the subsidy standard during most audit cycles.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This configuration uses a success probability of <m>p = 0.70</m> from the old scenario. With Colorado's realistic baseline rate of 35%, the mean must shift lower, indicating that qualifying is the minority outcome state rather than the majority default.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p><m>E(X) = 1.75</m> households, <m>\text{Var}(X) = 1.14</m>. This indicates that while you expect nearly 2 households to qualify on average, a variance close to the mean reveals high relative sample-to-sample volatility.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! For any valid discrete random variable following a standard Binomial distribution, we evaluate the summary moments using algebraic parameter shortcuts:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p><strong>Expected Value (Mean):</strong> <m>E(X) = n \cdot p = 5 \cdot 0.35 = 1.75</m> households.</p></li>
                        <li><p><strong>Variance:</strong> <m>\text{Var}(X) = n \cdot p \cdot (1 - p) = 5 \cdot 0.35 \cdot 0.65 = 1.1375 \approx 1.14</m>.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                    <p><strong>Strategic Interpretation:</strong> In a small sample of 5, an expected value of 1.75 paired with a variance of 1.14 tells auditors to expect massive cluster fluctuations. Because the variance is high relative to the mean, drawing a sample with absolutely 0 qualifiers or a sample where 4 out of 5 qualify are both standard operational occurrences, rather than highly unusual anomalies.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>E(X) = 1.75</m> households, <m>\text{Var}(X) = 1.75</m>. This indicates that the system behaves as a standard Poisson stream where the arrival count and dispersion are perfectly locked.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. While a Poisson distribution features an identical mean and variance (<m>\mu = \sigma^2</m>), a Binomial distribution's variance formula incorporates a regular dampening multiplier for failures, <m>(1 - p)</m>. Because <m>1 - 0.35 = 0.65 \lt 1</m>, the variance must be strictly smaller than the mean: <m>1.14 \lt 1.75</m>.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>E(X) = 2.50</m> households, <m>\text{Var}(X) = 1.25</m>. This indicates a perfectly symmetric, unskewed coin-tossing model where succeeding or failing to qualify are completely equally likely.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This matches a uniform or fair-coin system where <m>p = 0.50</m>. Because Colorado's actual conservation rate is lower (<m>p = 0.35</m>), the center of gravity shifts left and the distribution is right-skewed.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </task>
</exercise>


<exercise label="ex-binomial-drone-thrusters" xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org">
    <title>Binomial Distributions: Redundant Engineering Systems in Gaming Tech</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            During a championship sci-fi esports tournament, a media production company deploys an automated broadcast drone array to hover over the arena. To ensure stability, the drone's primary stabilization subsystem features a redundant bank of four identical hover-thrusters. Each individual thruster operates completely independently of the others.
        </p>
        <p>
            Due to the extreme heat during the live event match, each individual thruster has a probability of <m>p = 0.20</m> of overheating and failing before the 1,000-second match countdown timer finishes. Crucially, the stabilization subsystem is designed with fail-safes: it will keep the drone airborne if any two of the  four thrusters  remain operational.
        </p>

    </introduction>

    <!-- Part C: Exact Failures (Short Answer Entry) -->
    <task xml:id="task-drone-exact-failures-input">
<statement>
            <p>
                Calculate the  probability that  3 of the 4 thrusters will fail during the match.
            </p>
            
            <p>
                Probability (rounded to four decimal places) = <fillin  answer="0.0256" width="10"/>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <evaluation>
            <evaluate>
                <test correct="yes">
                    <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
                </test>
            </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We are asked to find the probability of experiencing exactly 3 failures across the 4 independent components. In terms of our random variable <m>X</m> (which tracks the number of *surviving* thrusters), this is mathematically equivalent to solving for the point probability of exactly 1 success:
                <me>P(\text{Exactly 3 Fail}) = P(X = 1)</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Evaluate this specific coordinate point using the standard Binomial probability mass function formula, where <m>n = 4</m>, <m>k = 1</m>, and the success probability is <m>p = 0.80</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>P(X = 1) \amp = \binom{4}{1} p^1 (1-p)^{n-1}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = \binom{4}{1} (0.80)^1 (0.20)^3</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp = 4 \cdot 0.80 \cdot 0.0080 = 0.0256</mrow>
                </md>
                The exact probability that exactly 3 thrusters fail is precisely <strong>0.0256</strong> (or a 2.56% chance). Notice that this matches the intermediate baseline component we isolated while setting up the complement subtraction loop in Part A!
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part A: Probability of Operation (Fill in the Blank) -->
    <task xml:id="task-drone-survival-probability">
        <statement>

            <p>
                Calculate the  probability that the drone stabilization subsystem successfully operates longer than the 1,000-second tournament window.
            </p>

            
        </statement>
       <choices>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>A. <m>0.1536</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This is the isolated point probability that exactly two components survive (<m>P(X = 2)</m>). The drone subsystem remains operational if <m>two, three, \text{ or } four</m> components function cleanly, so you must find the cumulative area for the entire interval.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>B. <m>0.0272</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This is the catastrophic failure rate (<m>P(X \lt 2)</m>), which counts the scenarios where either 0 or only 1 thruster stays online. Subtract this value from total certainty (1.00) to isolate the successful operating space.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>C. <m>0.9728</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! Because we have a fixed set of independent components (<m>n = 4</m>) where each has a constant binary success rate of remaining online (<m>p_{\text{success}} = 1 - 0.20 = 0.80</m>), the random count <m>X</m> follows a standard Binomial profile: <m>X \sim \text{Binomial}(n = 4, p = 0.80)</m>.</p>
                    <p>The system successfully operates if <m>X \ge 2</m>. Applying the <strong>Complement Rule</strong> makes this check highly efficient:</p>
                    <me>P(X \ge 2) = 1 - P(X \lt 2) = 1 - \left[ P(X = 0) + P(X = 1) \right]</me>
                    <p>Evaluating the individual failure states using the Binomial PMF:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Exactly 0 components survive: <m>\binom{4}{0} (0.80)^0 (0.20)^4 = 1 \cdot 1 \cdot 0.0016 = 0.0016</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Exactly 1 component survives: <m>\binom{4}{1} (0.80)^1 (0.20)^3 = 4 \cdot 0.80 \cdot 0.0080 = 0.0256</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>Summing these lower tail blocks and subtracting from 1 yields: <m>1 - (0.0016 + 0.0256) = 1 - 0.0272 = 0.9728</m>. The cluster completes its run with a 97.28% reliability rate.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>D. <m>0.8192</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This represents the probability that the system encounters at most one failure, but the calculation step forgot to include the perfect scenario where absolutely zero thrusters overheat (<m>P(X = 4)</m>).</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>E. <m>0.4096</m></p></statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This looks like the isolated point probability that exactly three components survive (<m>P(X = 3)</m>). Make sure to gather the full structural interval area across all operating states.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>

    </task>

    <!-- Part B: Summary Moments & Volatility Analysis (Multiple Choice) -->
    <task xml:id="task-drone-moments-mc">
        <statement>

            <p>
                Calculate the  expected number and the variance of surviving thrusters in this 4-element array block. Which option correctly identifies these values and maps out the true risk behavior of the tech array?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>E(X) = 0.80</m> thrusters, <m>\text{Var}(X) = 0.64</m>. This indicates that under normal conditions, almost all thrusters are expected to burn out immediately during the countdown launch sequence.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This option swaps the probability parameters, using the failure rate of 0.20 as the target success probability. Because each thruster has a dominant 80% chance of staying online, the expected survival count must be high.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement>
                    <p><m>E(X) = 3.20</m> thrusters, <m>\text{Var}(X) = 0.64</m>. This confirms that while the vast majority of parts stay operational on average, a low variance keeps the cluster highly clustered around safe operating levels.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Correct! For any valid discrete random variable following a standard Binomial distribution, we evaluate the summary moments using algebraic parameter shortcuts based on our success parameters (<m>n = 4</m>, <m>p = 0.80</m>):</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p><strong>Expected Value (Mean):</strong> <m>E(X) = n \cdot p = 4 \cdot 0.80 = 3.20</m> operational thrusters.</p></li>
                        <li><p><strong>Variance:</strong> <m>\text{Var}(X) = n \cdot p \cdot (1 - p) = 4 \cdot 0.80 \cdot 0.20 = 0.64</m>.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                    <p><strong>Operational Interpretation:</strong> An expected value of 3.20 proves that the system is engineered defensively. Since you only need 2 thrusters to survive, a mean of 3.20 paired with a very tight variance of 0.64 ensures that the random outcome is strongly squashed against the upper boundary, making a catastrophic drop down to 0 or 1 active thrusters an exceedingly rare edge case anomaly.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>E(X) = 3.20</m> thrusters, <m>\text{Var}(X) = 3.20</m>. This implies the system's failure patterns behave like a standard continuous Poisson decay field.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. While a Poisson system features a locked mean and variance, a Binomial distribution dampens its variance by multiplying by the failure weight, <m>(1-p)</m>. Since <m>0.20 \lt 1</m>, the variance must be strictly smaller than the mean: <m>0.64 \lt 3.20</m>.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement>
                    <p><m>E(X) = 2.00</m> thrusters, <m>\text{Var}(X) = 1.00</m>. This models a perfectly balanced system where turning on or burning out are entirely equally likely.</p>
                </statement>
                <feedback>
                    <p>Incorrect. This matches a fair coin model where <m>p = 0.50</m>. Because the components are high-grade and heavily biased toward survival (<m>p = 0.80</m>), the center of mass sits much further right.</p>
                </feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </task>
</exercise>

   </exercises>



<exercises xml:id="hw-binom-rv-turn-in" >
   <title>Written Exercises</title>
   



<exercise xml:id="ex-antenna-network-binomial">
    <title>Discrete Distributions: Smart Antenna Connectivity</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A high-performance mobile streaming device utilizes three redundant cellular data antennas acting independently of each other. The device succeeds in sending a data packet if any one or more of the antennas successfully establish a connection with the local network hub.
        </p>
        <p>
            During peak hours, each individual antenna possesses a baseline probability of <m>p = 0.8</m> of successfully establishing a network connection. Suppose the device attempts to send a packet during peak hours. Let the random variable <m>X_i</m> indicate whether antenna <m>i</m> successfully connects (for <m>i=1,2,3</m>), and let <m>Y = \sum_{i=1}^3 X_i</m> represent the total number of operational antennas.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    
    <hint>
        <p>
            
        </p>
    </hint>

    <!-- Part 1: Random Variable Identification -->
    <task xml:id="task-network-rv-classification">
        <statement>
            <p>
                What type of random variables are <m>X_i</m> (for <m>i=1,2,3</m>) and <m>Y</m>? Explain your reasoning.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Each individual variable <m>X_i</m> is a <term>Bernoulli random variable</term>. This is because each antenna represents a single isolated trial with exactly two outcomes: success (connecting with probability <m>0.8</m>) or failure (not connecting with probability <m>0.2</m>).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The total sum variable <m>Y</m> is a <term>Binomial random variable</term>. This is because it represents the total number of successes compiled across a fixed number (<m>n = 3</m>) of independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) Bernoulli trials.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 2: PMFs and Bar Charts -->
    <task xml:id="task-network-pmfs-and-graphs">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Specify the probability distribution functions (PMFs) for the random variables <m>X_i</m> and <m>Y</m>, plugging in the correct values for the parameter(s). Also sketch graphs (bar charts) of the resulting distributions.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The PMF for each individual Bernoulli variable <m>X_i</m> with parameter <m>p = 0.8</m> is defined as:
                <me>P(X_i = x) = 0.8^x(0.2)^{1-x} \quad \text{for } x \in \{0, 1\}</me>
                The PMF for the aggregate Binomial variable <m>Y</m> with parameters <m>n = 3</m> and <m>p = 0.8</m> uses the formula <m>P(Y = y) = \binom{3}{y}(0.8)^y(0.2)^{3-y}</m>, which yields:
                <ul>
                    <li><p><m>P(Y = 0) = \binom{3}{0}(0.8)^0(0.2)^3 = 1 \times 1 \times 0.008 = 0.008</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>P(Y = 1) = \binom{3}{1}(0.8)^1(0.2)^2 = 3 \times 0.8 \times 0.04 = 0.096</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>P(Y = 2) = \binom{3}{2}(0.8)^2(0.2)^1 = 3 \times 0.64 \times 0.2 = 0.384</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>P(Y = 3) = \binom{3}{3}(0.8)^3(0.2)^0 = 1 \times 0.512 \times 1 = 0.512</m></p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                Below are the rendered bar charts for both probability distributions:
            </p>
            <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
                <!-- Graph for Xi -->
                <image xml:id="tikz-network-xi-pmf">
                    <description>Bar chart of the Bernoulli PMF for Xi showing heights of 0.2 at x=0 and 0.8 at x=1.</description>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
                            \fill[white] (-1,-1) rectangle (3,5);
                            \draw[-&gt;,thick] (-0.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;,thick] (0,-0.2) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {$P(X_i=x)$};
                            \foreach \y/\l in {1/0.2, 2/0.4, 3/0.6, 4/0.8} {
                                \draw[gray!20,dashed] (-0.2,\y) -- (2.2,\y);
                                \draw (0.05,\y) -- (-0.05,\y) node[left,scale=0.8] {\l};
                            }
                            \draw (0,0.05) -- (0,-0.05) node[below,scale=0.8] {0};
                            \draw (1,0.05) -- (1,-0.05) node[below,scale=0.8] {1};
                            
                            \fill[blue!50,draw=blue,thick] (-0.2,0) rectangle (0.2,1);
                            \fill[blue!50,draw=blue,thick] (0.8,0) rectangle (1.2,4);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
                <!-- Graph for Y -->
                <image xml:id="tikz-network-y-pmf">
                    <description>Bar chart of the Binomial PMF for Y showing columns at y=0,1,2,3 with matching evaluated probability weights.</description>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
                            \fill[white] (-1,-1) rectangle (4.5,5.5);
                            \draw[-&gt;,thick] (-0.5,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$y$};
                            \draw[-&gt;,thick] (0,-0.2) -- (0,5.2) node[above] {$P(Y=y)$};
                            \foreach \y/\l in {1/0.1, 2/0.2, 3/0.3, 4/0.4, 5/0.5} {
                                \draw[gray!20,dashed] (-0.2,\y) -- (3.7,\y);
                                \draw (0.05,\y) -- (-0.05,\y) node[left,scale=0.8] {\l};
                            }
                            \foreach \x in {0,1,2,3} \draw (\x,0.05) -- (\x,-0.05) node[below,scale=0.8] {\x};
                            
                            \fill[red!50,draw=red,thick] (-0.15,0) rectangle (0.15,0.08);
                            \fill[red!50,draw=red,thick] (0.85,0) rectangle (1.15,0.96);
                            \fill[red!50,draw=red,thick] (1.85,0) rectangle (2.15,3.84);
                            \fill[red!50,draw=red,thick] (2.85,0) rectangle (3.15,5.12);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- Part 3: Cumulative Distribution Function -->
    <task xml:id="task-network-cdf-graph">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the cumulative distribution function (CDF) for the random variable <m>Y</m> and sketch its resulting graph (step function).
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The cumulative distribution function <m>F_Y(y) = P(Y \le y)</m> accumulates the discrete probability steps step-by-step:
                <me>
                    F_Y(y) = \begin{cases} 
                    0 \amp \text{for } y \lt 0 \\ 
                    0.008 \amp \text{for } 0 \le y \lt 1 \\ 
                    0.104 \amp \text{for } 1 \le y \lt 2 \\ 
                    0.488 \amp \text{for } 2 \le y \lt 3 \\ 
                    1.000 \amp \text{for } y \ge 3 
                    \end{cases}
                </me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Below is the plotted step-graph representing the cumulative distribution function:
            </p>
            <image xml:id="tikz-network-y-cdf">
                <description>A step function plot showing flat lines with solid starting circles and open ending circles at each discrete threshold point.</description>
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
                        \fill[white] (-1.5,-1) rectangle (5,6);
                        \draw[-&gt;,thick] (-1,0) -- (4.5,0) node[right] {$y$};
                        \draw[-&gt;,thick] (0,-0.2) -- (0,5.5) node[above] {$F_Y(y)$};
                        
                        \draw (0.05,0.08) -- (-0.05,0.08) node[left,scale=0.7] {0.008};
                        \draw (0.05,1.04) -- (-0.05,1.04) node[left,scale=0.7] {0.104};
                        \draw (0.05,2.44) -- (-0.05,2.44) node[left,scale=0.7] {0.488};
                        \draw (0.05,5) -- (-0.05,5) node[left,scale=0.7] {1.0};
                        
                        \foreach \x in {1,2,3} \draw (\x,0.05) -- (\x,-0.05) node[below,scale=0.8] {\x};
                        
                        \draw[purple,very thick] (-1,0) -- (0,0);
                        \fill[white] (0,0) circle (2pt); \draw[purple] (0,0) circle (2pt);
                        
                        \draw[purple,very thick] (0,0.08) -- (1,0.08);
                        \fill[purple] (0,0.08) circle (2pt);
                        \fill[white] (1,0.08) circle (2pt); \draw[purple] (1,0.08) circle (2pt);
                        
                        \draw[purple,very thick] (1,1.04) -- (2,1.04);
                        \fill[purple] (1,1.04) circle (2pt);
                        \fill[white] (2,1.04) circle (2pt); \draw[purple] (2,1.04) circle (2pt);
                        
                        \draw[purple,very thick] (2,2.44) -- (3,2.44);
                        \fill[purple] (2,2.44) circle (2pt);
                        \fill[white] (3,2.44) circle (2pt); \draw[purple] (3,2.44) circle (2pt);
                        
                        \draw[purple,very thick] (3,5) -- (4.2,5);
                        \fill[purple] (3,5) circle (2pt);
                        
\end{tikzpicture}
